From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dan on Selwyn <dan@awto.au>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 15:51:41 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: designware: allow opting out of IC_ENABLE.ABORT and the
 forced SDA RX hold

Two DT opt-outs for controllers whose targets cannot tolerate mainline's
defaults.

snps,no-enable-abort: __i2c_dw_disable() writes IC_ENABLE.ABORT when the master
is holding the bus, which terminates the transfer MID-BYTE. A Seiko S-35390A
RTC answers that (datasheet Fig 46) by holding SDA low until clocked through -
an unrecoverable bus wedge needing a power cycle, since the DW v1.20 core has
no SDA-stuck recovery and this board's SCL/SDA have no GPIO alternate for a
bit-bang deblock.

Introduced by 2409205acd3c (2023). The vendor 4.19 kernel this board shipped
with has no abort at all and never wedged; nor does its U-Boot, whose RTC
access works on identical hardware. Reachable on every transfer, not just error
paths: i2c_dw_xfer_init() calls __i2c_dw_disable(), and emptyfifo_hold_master
is true here.

snps,no-sda-rx-hold: 171e23e150ac (2016) force-sets a non-zero SDA RX hold as
an admittedly empirical fix ("Reason for this is unknown but after trial and
error") for one Intel platform, applied unconditionally to every DesignWare
controller since. Provided for controllers that need the register to match a
known-good vendor configuration exactly.

Both default to current behaviour; only a DT opting in changes anything.

Tested on a Ubiquiti UNVR (Annapurna Labs Alpine V2): a full i2cdetect scan of
the RTC's mux channel completes with zero timeouts where it previously wedged
the bus for every subsequent transfer.
---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-common.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-common.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-common.c
index 5cefbc66b9e7..84309bd5fd93 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-common.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-designware-common.c
@@ -489,7 +489,8 @@ int i2c_dw_set_sda_hold(struct dw_i2c_dev *dev)
 		 * extends incoming SDA low to high transition while SCL is
 		 * high but it appears to help also above issue.
 		 */
-		if (!(dev->sda_hold_time & DW_IC_SDA_HOLD_RX_MASK))
+		if (!(dev->sda_hold_time & DW_IC_SDA_HOLD_RX_MASK) &&
+		    !device_property_read_bool(dev->dev, "snps,no-sda-rx-hold"))
 			dev->sda_hold_time |= 1 << DW_IC_SDA_HOLD_RX_SHIFT;
 
 		dev_dbg(dev->dev, "SDA Hold Time TX:RX = %d:%d\n",
@@ -525,6 +526,17 @@ void __i2c_dw_disable(struct dw_i2c_dev *dev)
 
 	abort_needed = (raw_intr_stats & DW_IC_INTR_MST_ON_HOLD) ||
 			(ic_stats & DW_IC_STATUS_MASTER_HOLD_TX_FIFO_EMPTY);
+
+	/*
+	 * IC_ENABLE.ABORT terminates the transfer mid-byte, which some targets
+	 * (S-35390A, Fig 46) answer by holding SDA low until clocked through -
+	 * an unrecoverable bus wedge. Introduced in 2023 (2409205acd3c); the
+	 * vendor 4.19 kernel that drives this board has no abort and does not
+	 * wedge. Opt out per-controller.
+	 */
+	if (device_property_read_bool(dev->dev, "snps,no-enable-abort"))
+		abort_needed = false;
+
 	if (abort_needed) {
 		if (!(enable & DW_IC_ENABLE_ENABLE)) {
 			regmap_write(dev->map, DW_IC_ENABLE, DW_IC_ENABLE_ENABLE);
